<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>SshWarden.OAuth</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    OAuth 2.1 for any authorization server, which is the whole reason this exists beside
    SshWarden.Boltway.

    Boltway was the first implementation and it is a fine one, but it is a sibling project: a
    deployment on Keycloak, Entra, Auth0 or anything else was told, by the error message this
    package's absence produced, to install another project's NuGet packages. For a server whose
    selling point is that it validates somebody else's tokens, "works with one authorization server"
    is the wrong answer.

    So: Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer, which every .NET reader already knows, doing
    OpenID Connect discovery against the configured issuer. What it does not do is RFC 9728 - that
    document is the resource server's own, not the authorization server's, and it is sixty lines
    written here rather than a dependency.

    SshWarden.Boltway stays for a deployment that wants Boltway's own reading of a token, and
    nothing in this repository requires either one: both fill ISshWardenAuthenticator, which is the
    seam, and a deployment picks one or writes a third.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../SshWarden.Mcp/SshWarden.Mcp.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
